## Why This Is Asked
Tests involving real-time notifications with WebSockets and a message broker are common in production systems. This question evaluates practical measurement skills rather than theoretical definitions.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket concurrency management
- End-to-end latency and tail latency (p95/p99)
- Backpressure and buffering strategies
- Throughput vs. latency tradeoffs
- Observability across brokers and worker pools

## Code Example
```javascript
// WebSocket load test using k6
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  vus: 100000,
  duration: '5m',
  thresholds: {
    'ws_latency_p95': ['p(95)<500'],
    'ws_latency_p99': ['p(99)<1000'],
  },
};

export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://notification-service/ws';
  const res = ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('message', (data) => {
      socket.markPing();
    });
    socket.on('close', () => {});
    socket.setTimeout(() => {
      socket.close();
    }, 30000);
  });
  check(res, { 'status is 101': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Metrics to Capture
| Metric | Target | Warning |
|--------|--------|--------|
| End-to-end latency p95 | <500ms | >500ms |
| End-to-end latency p99 | <1s | >1s |
| Kafka consumer lag | <1000 | >1000 |
| WebSocket reconnect rate | <1% | >1% |
| Worker pool utilization | <80% | >80% |

## Workload Model
- **Steady state**: 50k concurrent connections, 10 events/sec
- **Burst**: Scale to 100k connections, 1000 events/sec over 30 seconds
- **Cool-down**: Return to steady state over 60 seconds

## Failure Scenarios
1. **Kafka broker failure**: Restart broker, measure consumer group rebalance time
2. **Worker pool exhaustion**: Artificially limit pool size, observe backpressure
3. **Network partition**: Disconnect 20% of clients, verify graceful reconnection

